* general ncurses user interface

We must start thinking a little more generally of the ncurses user interface.

I want that clautolisp starts in the dumb terminal mode REPL.

We have an inspector (only in the debugger for now, we need to provide
a public API for it: (clal-inspect object)), and an editor
(clal-sedit) that work in the dumb terminal.

We have the debugger working in the dumb terminal.

And with cadtui, we're getting a CAD working in the dumb terminal
too.

Now, when we want to benefit from the 2D terminal capabilities with
ncurses or PDcurses, perhaps we would want to do it for all these
features.  Notably in conjuction with cadtui, which introduces the
notion of window, menus, and views.  So far cadtui is  envisionned to
run on the dumb terminal, but it would also be a good match to a 2D
terminal with ncurses/PDcurses.

Now, we won't be re-implementing a whole emacs, but this user
interface would be implemented as A mini emacs. Ie. we provide some
mechanisms, and built-in commands, but the user may define his own
commands.  built-in commands are written in Common Lisp, and
user-defined commands are written in autolisp, and can be hooked to
key bindings to be invoked from the ncurses TUI, or they can be
invoked by command name or abbreviation in the dumb UI (as we already
specified it).

We'll take the notion of frame from emacs. A frame is a set of windows
that is displayed in a GUI unit (what a GUI usually calls a window). A
dumb terminal is a frame containing a single window. A 2D terminal is
a frame that can display with ncurses several windows.

* frames

So our systems starts up with a single dumb-terminal frame, and we may
create new frames when needed.

| name           | description                               |
|----------------+-------------------------------------------|
| tty-frame      | dumb terminal frame                       |
| ncurses-frame  | ncurses 2D terminal frame                 |
| PDcurses-frame | PDcurses 2D terminal frame                |
| vdt-frame      | abstract, ncurses-frame or PDcurses-frame |

To hide the implementation details, we could call both ncurses-frame
and PDcurses-frame vdt-frame (Video Display Terminal, assuming that we
skip directly to cursor addressable and smart terminals (since we can
do ncurses on it)).

All the functions defined here are prefixed by clal- when exposed to
autolisp.
All the object types defined here are opaque types.

** (frame-list) --> list of frame

Initially, only one tty-frame instance, representing stdin/stdout

** (selected-frame) --> frame

return the currently active frame

** (make-frame options)

options is a list of:

- (name . STRING)	The frame should be named STRING.
- (width . NUMBER)	The frame should be NUMBER characters in width.
- (height . NUMBER)	The frame should be NUMBER text lines high.
- (minibuffer . t)	The frame should have a minibuffer.
- (minibuffer . nil)	The frame should have no minibuffer.
- (device . tty)	The frame should be displayed on a dumb terminal device.
- (device . vdt)	The frame should be displayed on a 2D terminal device.

We could have several tty-frame on the list. Only the selected frame
is working. For the dumb terminal this wouldn't make a difference,
since all tty-frame share the same terminal flow, but logically we'd
have a different context and if later we work with multiple devices,
this could be differentiated.

We will definitely have several vdt-frame, and selecting one or
another would redisplay the screen. Selecting back a tty-frame would
swap the terminal back on the tty page.

** (frame-name frame) -> string
** (set-frame-name frame string)
** (frame-width frame) -> integer
** (frame-height frame) -> integer
** (set-frame-width frame integer)
** (set-frame-height frame integer)
** (frame-minibuffer frame) -> nil or buffer
** (frame-device frame) -> (member tty vdt)

** (delete-frame frame)

Remove the frame from the frame list and deletes it.
Nothing should use this frame anymore.

** (select-frame frame)

Selects the frame.

When a tty-frame is selected, the terminals goes back to the initial
page, using normal tty settings and line discipline.

When a vdt-frame is selected, the terminal goes to 2D ncurses/PDcurses
mode. If the frame size is less than the terminal size, then a border
may be displayed centered in the terminal (with the background
unchanged). Otherwise the whole terminal size is used (the frame size
is updated).

When the terminal size changes (SIGWINCH) the frame size should be
updated too.

** (terminal-device-supports-vdt-p) -> boolean

indicates whether we can create and use a vdt-frame.

* windows

frames may contain one or more windows. One of the window of a frame
can be a special window called the ~*minibuffer*~ which is used to
input commands and arguments. Only vdt-frame can meaningfully have a
~*minibuffer*~ window, which is always 1 single line at the bottom of
the frame. tty-frame have an implicit minibuffer since they use line
discipline.

Windows can be laid out horizontally and/or vertically in the frame
and they always take the whole space of the frame.  Windows usually
have a status line at the bottom of the window, which display the
title of the window and other status information.  The active window
has a status line displayed is a special face different from the face
used for inactive windows to let the user identify it clearly.

A face is a set of display parameters (bold, underline, italic,
blinking, inverted, foreground color, background color), and are
configurable.

** (make-window options) -> window

options is a list of options
- (name . "string") the name/title of the window

The window is created in the selected frame.  

If the selected frame has no window so far, it speads over the whole
frame, with layout:
#+begin_example
(whole <window-index>)
#+end_example

If there is already a minibuffer window, then the layout becomes:
#+begin_example
(horizontal <window-index> <minibuffer-index>)
#+end_example

If there is already a single non-minibuffer window, then the layout
becomes:
#+begin_example
(horizontal <previous-window-index> <window-index>)
#+end_example

Note: the minibuffer is always in:
#+begin_src 
(horizontal … <minibuffer-index>)
#+end_src

If there are already windows, then the layout becomes:
#+begin_example
(horizontal (horizontal … <window-index>) <minibuffer-index>)
#+end_example

** (window-name window) --> string
** (set-window-name window string)
** (window-list frame minibufp)

frame = nil list all the windows in all the frame. Otherwise only list
the windows in the given frame. (Note: the tty-frame has one repl
window, and no minibuffer window).

minibufp = nil: don't return the minibuffers. = t: includes the
minibuffers in the resulting list.

** (delete-window window)

Removes the window from the frame. This reorganizes the layout.

** (selected-window) --> window

Returns the active window in the selected frame.

** (select-window window)

Activates the window.
Make window’s frame the selected frame and WINDOW that frame’s
selected window.


* example of operation

We start in the tty-frame "autolisp", the initial frame.

If we run ~(clal-sedit 'foo)~, we edit the function foo, in the
tty-frame, with the line commands.



But we may also run: ~(call-with-temp-window 'clal-sedit '(foo))~
or: ~(with-temp-window (lambda (window) (clal-sedit 'foo)))~

#+begin_src lisp
  (let ((temp-frame (make-frame '((name . "temp") (device . vdt) (minibuffer . t)))))
         ;; we may use the minibuffer for M-x and , commands
    (select-frame temp-frame)
    (let ((temp-window (make-window '((name . "temp")))))
      (select-window temp-window)
      (prog1 (clal-sedit 'foo)
         (delete-frame temp-frame)))) ; this deletes the windows inside the frame.
#+end_src

in which case, sedit will use the features of the 2D terminal and the
window interactor will process char discipline input and mapping to
key to commands.

The important aspect is that we are using ncurses/PDcurses temporarily
in the middle of a dumb terminal session.

The same mechanism is used by aldo when the --debuging-ui
ncurses/PDcurses option is given.

* ATTENTION - no threads

ATTENTION: We don't have multiple threads, so displaying and updating
of window, and command input and processing (by interactors, or REPL
input), is still done by the single thread, In the example above, by
the clal-sedit function. It must be programmed explicitely to install
its key binding or commands in an interactor and stack it to benefit
from the possibility of calling the underlying interactor ie. the Lisp
REPL, while editing. The window manager can process input only when
the function calls the autolisp READ-LINE or READ-CHAR (cf cadtui
design, we will have to reconcile both views but we need to use the
same mechanism here, since we have no threads).

Actually, this is part of what an emacs is: the "applications" are
implemented as a set of independent commands put in dispatching
tables, like we have in the case of the debugger. It's a single
interactor loop that gets the commands and dispatch them to the right
function, logically to inspect, sedit, navi, aldo or lisp. The
addition of windows doesn't change this. If we created a frame with
eg. a REPL and two sedit on two different files, to make things going,
we would have to assemble one stack of interactor for each window, one
with the LISP interactor for the REPL window, and two stacks with
sedit interactors but with different states! (cf aldo, IIRC we had to
deal with this state for the interactors).
In the example above, clal-sedit must deal with the interactors
explicitely both in the tty case and in the vdt case using the window
parameter (actually (selected-window)).

* stack

So we'll need a new stack interactor.

Les frames sont actuellement affichés seulement avec le nom
de la fonction et la ligne où se trouve l'appel, je suppose.

Je m'attendrais à avoir pour les frames d'appel de fonction, outre le
nom de la fonction, les arguments (prin1'ed avec des restrictions
~*print-length* ~=3~ ~*print-length* ~=1~ etc (mais aussi configurable)).

En navigant sur les frames on peut les "ouvrir" pour afficher les
bindings locaux. (et les refermer).

La commande d'évaluation se fait dans le scope de la frame
sélectionnée.

Il  faut aussi  prévoir une  protection, dans  le cas  où il  y a  une
recursivités trés  profonde, ne pas  afficher des milliers  de frames.
S'arrêter à un nombre raisonnable (configurable), et si on actionne la dernière
ligne, on ajoute des frames  supplémentaires (une indication du nombre
total de frames serait utile).

Voici un exemple du backtrace dans ~*sldb*~:

#+begin_example
Backtrace:
  0: (ccl::call-check-regs break "hi")
      Locals:
        fn = break
        args = ("hi")
        old-regs = nil
  1: (ccl::cheap-eval (break "hi"))
  2: (swank::eval-region "(break \"hi\")
     ")
      Locals:
        string = "(break \"hi\")
                 "
        stream = #<string-input-stream  #x302002B3DCFD>
        values = nil
        - = (break "hi")
        form = (break "hi")
  3: ((:internal swank-repl::repl-eval))
  4: (swank-repl::track-package #<ccl:compiled-lexical-closure (:internal swank-repl::repl-eval) #x302002B3DD2F>)
  5: ((:internal swank-repl::repl-eval))
  6: (swank::call-with-retry-restart "Retry SLIME REPL evaluation request." #<ccl:compiled-lexical-closure (:internal swank-r..
  7: ((:internal swank-repl::repl-eval))
#+end_example

Voici les commandes de base fournies par sldb, pour s'en inspirer dans
la fenêtre stack, et dans aldb par la suite.

#+begin_example
Commands to examine the selected frame:
   t   - toggle details (local bindings, CATCH tags)
   v   - view source for the frame (in the source window)
   e   - eval in frame
   d   - eval in frame, pretty-print result
   D   - disassemble
   i   - inspect

Commands to invoke restarts:   (pass thru to the underlying interactor = aldo)
   q   - quit
   a   - abort
   c   - continue
   0-9 - restart shortcuts
   I   - invoke restart by name

Commands to navigate frames:
   n   - down
   p   - up
   M-n - down, with details
   M-p - up, with details
   TAB - cycle between restarts & backtrace   -- no restart in autolisp
   <   - beginning of backtrace
   >   - end of backtrace                     -- of displayed backtrace, We can type RET to
                                              -- expand when there are more frames below.
#+end_example

